# Structurizr Lite staging (built by `make c4-render`)
.structurizr/
.claude/settings.local.json
# Rust codegen build artifacts (ADR-0034)
tools/codegen-rs/target/
target/
# Ephemeral codegen scratch (e.g. `make c4-export` .mmd output)
tools/codegen-rs/out/

# Agent worktrees (temporary, auto-managed)
.claude/worktrees/

# Agent worktrees created inside the repo. Sessions have repeatedly made these as
# `wt2/`, `wt-main/`, `wt7/` ... An in-repo worktree shows up as untracked, and a
# `git add -A` would commit an entire second checkout into the tree. Ignoring them
# makes that mistake unspellable rather than merely discouraged. Prefer /tmp for a
# worktree; this is the safety net for when someone does not.
wt*/

# Register-check hook firing log (runtime artifact, ADR-20260821-010543)
.claude/register-check.log

# QMD advisory retrieval cache — derived, disposable, never authoritative (row RETRIEVAL-QMD-CI)
.qmd/

# Python bytecode caches — a .pyc with no source in this tree was committed once by an over-broad
# `git add` (review #8 of PR #679). Kept for the reason PR #679 landed it, and the reason is now
# WIDER than it was: that PR's comment said "Nothing in-tree runs a .py FILE today: every call site
# passes the program on the COMMAND LINE (`python3 -c`) or on STDIN", which was true of `main` and
# is FALSE of this tree. `.github/scripts/assert_review_marker.py`, its 73-case battery and the
# differential harness are .py FILES, invoked as files, and the battery runs in CI — so the
# interpreter can write bytecode for the imported module rather than only into a `mktemp -d`
# fixture some EXIT trap removes. Merging the branch that adds them is exactly when that sentence
# had to move, and this entry stops being insurance and starts being load-bearing.
#
# The rest of #679's reasoning stands and is worth keeping: the `PYTHONPATH=` probes in
# `stub-tests.sh` DO write `__pycache__/sitecustomize.*.pyc`, into a fixture their trap removes.
# An earlier version of that comment said "both Python call sites here use `python3 -c`" — there
# are ~20 across the gate scripts, two of them stdin heredocs, and the `PYTHONPATH` six do write
# bytecode; the conclusion held and every stated reason for it was wrong (review #83).
__pycache__/
*.pyc
